1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,088
This is STAT 331, video 19. It follows Lecture 9: categorical covariates, and hypothesis testing.

2
00:00:09,538 --> 00:00:31,213
After this video, you can put a categorical covariate into a regression model with indicator variables. You can interpret the parameters of two parameterizations. You can compare two groups with a t test, and all groups with an F test. You can derive the F statistic, and solve the two practice questions of Lecture 9.

3
00:00:31,663 --> 00:00:50,031
First, a short recap. The model is y equals X beta plus epsilon. The errors are multivariate normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared times I. The least squares estimate is X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y.

4
00:00:50,481 --> 00:01:12,710
beta hat has a normal distribution with mean beta, and variance sigma squared times X transpose X inverse. So one estimate, beta j hat, is normal with mean beta j, and variance sigma squared times V j j. V j j is the diagonal entry j j of X transpose X inverse.

5
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:27,411
Replace sigma by sigma hat. Then beta j hat minus beta j, over sigma hat times the square root of V j j, has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

6
00:01:27,861 --> 00:01:43,199
From this t result, we get a confidence interval for beta j, and a test of beta j equal to theta 0. The p-value is the probability that the absolute value of T is at least the absolute observed t.

7
00:01:43,649 --> 00:01:54,935
The same steps give a confidence interval for the mean response, and a prediction interval for a new y. This video adds one new type of covariate, and one new test.

8
00:01:55,385 --> 00:02:06,734
The slide asks for the meaning of beta j. Fix all covariates except x j. Then the mean of y is a straight line in x j. The green line shows it.

9
00:02:07,184 --> 00:02:22,416
Increase x j by one unit, from a to a plus 1. The mean of y changes by beta j. So beta j is the difference in mean y for a one-unit increase in x j, with all other covariates fixed.

10
00:02:22,866 --> 00:02:38,418
Fix the other covariates at different values. The line moves, but its slope stays beta j. So this interpretation needs a covariate where one unit has a meaning. The next slides show a covariate where this is not true.

11
00:02:38,868 --> 00:02:56,767
Here is the data example of Lecture 9. The file fishermen mercury dot c s v has 135 villagers from Kuwait. The outcome is MeHg: the concentration of methyl mercury in a hair sample, in milligrams per gram.

12
00:02:57,217 --> 00:03:20,129
One covariate is fishpart: the parts of fish that a subject eats. Code 0 is none, N. Code 1 is muscle tissue only, M. Code 2 is muscle tissue and sometimes the whole fish, MW. Code 3 is the whole fish, W. The table shows the first rows from the slide. We also use weight, in kilograms.

13
00:03:20,579 --> 00:03:36,643
fishpart is a categorical covariate. A categorical covariate puts each subject into one of a few categories, or levels. The plot shows MeHg for each level. The group sizes are 10, 28, 88, and 9.

14
00:03:37,093 --> 00:03:48,165
The purple marks are the group means. Group N has a much lower mean than the other three groups. The next slides show how to put such a covariate into a regression model.

15
00:03:48,615 --> 00:04:00,369
The first idea is to use the code as a number. The model is MeHg i equals beta 0, plus beta 1 times fishpart i, plus epsilon i.

16
00:04:00,819 --> 00:04:10,121
Then the mean of MeHg is a straight line in the code. The four groups sit on this line, at the codes 0, 1, 2, and 3.

17
00:04:10,571 --> 00:04:32,523
beta 1 is the mean difference for a one-unit increase in fishpart. A one-unit increase can be N to M, M to MW, or MW to W. All three steps have the same mean difference, beta 1. This is a strong assumption. Nothing in the data tells us that the categories are equally spaced.

18
00:04:32,973 --> 00:04:39,949
Pause the video and try it. Write each mean difference between neighbouring categories, for two different codes.

19
00:04:40,399 --> 00:04:52,857
With the code 0, 1, 2, 3, the means are beta 0, beta 0 plus beta 1, beta 0 plus 2 beta 1, and beta 0 plus 3 beta 1.

20
00:04:53,307 --> 00:04:56,657
Subtract neighbours. Each difference is beta 1.

21
00:04:57,107 --> 00:05:06,045
Now code N as 0, M as 10, MW as 11, and W as 15. These numbers are only labels too.

22
00:05:06,495 --> 00:05:13,685
The means are on the same kind of line. But the categories are now at 0, 10, 11, and 15.

23
00:05:14,135 --> 00:05:33,633
Subtract neighbours again. M minus N is 10 beta 1. MW minus M is beta 1. W minus MW is 4 beta 1. So the code alone changes the assumption about the mean differences. Each choice is equally strong, and the data did not choose it.

24
00:05:34,083 --> 00:05:54,009
We want a model with no assumption about the mean differences. Each category gets its own normal distribution. For category N, MeHg is normal with mean gamma N and variance sigma squared. The same holds for M, MW, and W, with the same sigma squared.

25
00:05:54,459 --> 00:06:03,803
The picture shows the four distributions. Each one has its own mean, gamma N, gamma M, gamma M W, and gamma W.

26
00:06:04,253 --> 00:06:19,506
To write this as a regression, we use the indicator function. I of A is 1 if the statement A is true, and 0 if it is false. The model has one indicator for each category, and each indicator has its own gamma.

27
00:06:19,956 --> 00:06:34,335
Put in each category. For a subject in N, only I of N is 1, so the mean is gamma N. For M, only I of M is 1, so the mean is gamma M. The same holds for MW and W.

28
00:06:34,785 --> 00:06:46,006
So each gamma is the mean of one category. The model makes no assumption about the differences between the four means. The indicators are also called dummy variables.

29
00:06:46,456 --> 00:06:56,803
Here is another parameterization of the same model. It has an intercept beta 0, and indicators for M, MW, and W only.

30
00:06:57,253 --> 00:07:19,205
Put in each category. For N, all three indicators are 0, so the mean is beta 0. For M, the mean is beta 0 plus beta M. For MW, it is beta 0 plus beta M W. For W, it is beta 0 plus beta W. The purple dashed line is the level beta 0.

31
00:07:19,655 --> 00:07:36,103
So beta M is the mean of M minus the mean of N. beta M W and beta W are the differences from N in the same way. The arrows show them. This model also makes no assumption about the differences between categories.

32
00:07:36,553 --> 00:07:48,798
There is no indicator for N. N is the referent category, also called the baseline category. Its mean is beta 0. Every other beta is a difference from the baseline.

33
00:07:49,248 --> 00:07:54,709
The slide asks: how are gamma and beta related? Pause the video and try it.

34
00:07:55,159 --> 00:08:18,583
Both parameterizations give the mean of each category. So set the means equal. gamma N equals beta 0. gamma M equals beta 0 plus beta M, and so on. Solve for beta. beta 0 is gamma N, and beta M is gamma M minus gamma N. beta M W and beta W follow in the same way.

35
00:08:19,033 --> 00:08:29,935
So both parameterizations have four parameters for the four category means. They describe the same model. We can always get one set of parameters from the other.

36
00:08:30,385 --> 00:08:42,310
Now add weight to the model. Slide 20 asks for a model where mean MeHg is linear in weight for each level of fishpart, with a common slope but different intercepts.

37
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:59,827
Add weight to either parameterization. In the first one, each category has its own gamma, and gamma 1 is the coefficient of weight. In the second one, beta 0 and the three betas give the levels, and beta 1 is the coefficient of weight.

38
00:09:00,277 --> 00:09:10,901
Here is the fit of the second model to the data. There is one line for each category. The lines are parallel, because all four groups share the slope of weight.

39
00:09:11,351 --> 00:09:23,596
The estimated common slope is 0.1511. So in each group, mean MeHg increases by about 1.5 for each 10 kilograms of weight.

40
00:09:24,046 --> 00:09:42,393
Interpret the first parameterization. Pause the video and try it. For a subject in N, the mean is gamma N plus gamma 1 times weight. For M, MW, and W, use gamma M, gamma M W, or gamma W in place of gamma N.

41
00:09:42,843 --> 00:10:04,709
Put weight equal to 0. Then gamma N is the mean MeHg in group N at weight 0. The other gammas are the same for their groups. The plot extends the lines to weight 0. The dots at the left are the four estimates. Weight 0 is far outside the data, so these intercepts have no practical meaning.

42
00:10:05,159 --> 00:10:19,431
gamma 1 is the difference in mean MeHg for a one kilogram increase in weight, within one level x of fishpart. It is the same at every weight a, and in every group, because the lines are parallel.

43
00:10:19,881 --> 00:10:31,487
Now the second parameterization. For N, the intercept is beta 0. For M, it is beta 0 plus beta M. The slope is beta 1 in every group.

44
00:10:31,937 --> 00:10:45,078
beta 0 is the mean MeHg in group N at weight 0. beta 1 is the mean difference for a one kilogram increase in weight, in any group. It is the same as gamma 1.

45
00:10:45,528 --> 00:11:08,483
beta M is the difference in mean MeHg between group M and group N, at the same weight a. The lines are parallel, so this gap is the same at every weight. The arrows show beta M, beta M W, and beta W at three different weights. We say: beta M is the difference between M and N, adjusted for weight.

46
00:11:08,933 --> 00:11:26,661
The estimates are 3.081 for beta M, 2.239 for beta M W, and 3.374 for beta W. So, at the same weight, the groups that eat fish have higher mean MeHg than group N.

47
00:11:27,111 --> 00:11:38,012
Both parameterizations make the same assumptions about the data. So we can get the parameters of one from the other. They only differ in their natural interpretation.

48
00:11:38,462 --> 00:11:59,539
Next, we test whether mean MeHg varies by fishpart, with weight held constant. The null hypothesis says that the four groups have the same mean at each weight. In the first form, the four gammas are equal. In the second form, beta M, beta M W, and beta W are all 0.

49
00:11:59,989 --> 00:12:06,667
Under this null hypothesis, the four lines become one line. The gray dashed line shows it.

50
00:12:07,117 --> 00:12:16,098
The second form sets parameters equal to 0. This is simpler to test. So from here on, we use the model with an intercept.

51
00:12:16,548 --> 00:12:30,841
First, test whether the mean of M equals the mean of N, at the same weight a. Write the difference with the parameters. The beta 0 terms and the beta 1 a terms cancel. The difference is beta M.

52
00:12:31,291 --> 00:12:56,230
So the null hypothesis is beta M equals 0. This is one parameter, so the test is the usual t test. The model has p plus 1 equal to 5 parameters, so the degrees of freedom are 135 minus 5, which is 130. The red tails show the 5 percent rejection region. The critical value is 1.978.

53
00:12:56,680 --> 00:13:13,192
From the data, beta M hat is 3.0815, and its standard error is 0.9533. So the observed t is 3.233. The two-sided p-value is 0.0016.

54
00:13:13,642 --> 00:13:26,634
The p-value is less than 0.05, so we reject the null hypothesis. At the same weight, the mean MeHg of group M is different from the mean of group N.

55
00:13:27,084 --> 00:13:38,561
Next, test whether the means of M and MW are equal, at the same weight. Neither group is the referent. Pause the video and try to write the null hypothesis.

56
00:13:39,011 --> 00:13:53,177
Write the difference with the parameters. beta 0 and beta 1 a cancel again. The difference is beta M minus beta M W. In the picture, it is the red gap between the blue and the teal lines.

57
00:13:53,627 --> 00:14:07,707
So the null hypothesis is beta M equals beta M W. This is the same as beta M minus beta M W equal to 0. It is a linear combination of the parameters.

58
00:14:08,157 --> 00:14:26,503
beta M hat minus beta M W hat is a linear combination of the normal vector beta hat. So it is normal. Subtract the null value 0, and divide by its standard deviation. The result is standard normal. We need the variance of the difference.

59
00:14:26,953 --> 00:14:38,132
The variance of a difference is the variance of the first, plus the variance of the second, minus 2 times their covariance. The green cells give the two variances.

60
00:14:38,582 --> 00:15:01,750
The variance matrix of beta hat is sigma squared times V. Number its rows from 1, in the order beta 0, beta 1, beta M, beta M W, beta W. So the variances are sigma squared V 3 3 and sigma squared V 4 4. The covariance is sigma squared V 3 4, the orange cells.

61
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:25,005
Replace sigma squared by sigma hat squared. For the data, sigma hat squared is 6.3695. The V entries give 0.05049. So the variance is 0.3216, and the standard error is 0.5671. These numbers are computed from the data.

62
00:15:25,455 --> 00:15:52,314
Then compare with the t distribution with 130 degrees of freedom. The observed t is 0.8422 over 0.5671, which is 1.485. The p-value is 0.140. So we do not reject the null hypothesis. The data give no evidence that groups M and MW differ in mean MeHg, at the same weight.

63
00:15:52,764 --> 00:16:02,236
Now the main question: whether mean MeHg varies by fishpart, adjusted for weight. The parameter vector has five entries.

64
00:16:02,686 --> 00:16:20,414
Let beta star be the vector of beta M, beta M W, and beta W. Its length q is 3. We test the null hypothesis beta star equals 0, against the two-sided alternative, beta star not equal to 0.

65
00:16:20,864 --> 00:16:40,171
This null hypothesis is different from the ones we saw before. It does not set one parameter, or one linear combination, equal to a number. It sets three parameters to 0 together. This is a joint null hypothesis. A t test cannot do this. We need the F test.

66
00:16:40,621 --> 00:17:05,005
As before, we measure how far the estimate is from 0, relative to its uncertainty. beta hat is normal. So the sub-vector beta star hat is normal too, with mean beta star, and variance sigma squared V star. V star is the sub-matrix of X transpose X inverse for the entries of beta star. It is the purple 3 by 3 block.

67
00:17:05,455 --> 00:17:17,252
Define the F statistic. It is beta star hat transpose, times V star inverse, times beta star hat. Divide this by q times sigma hat squared.

68
00:17:17,702 --> 00:17:29,457
We will show that under the null hypothesis, F has an F distribution, with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. q is the length of beta star.

69
00:17:29,907 --> 00:17:44,200
With one parameter, this is the squared estimate over its variance. With q parameters, V star inverse also accounts for the correlations between the estimates. The next slides derive the distribution.

70
00:17:44,650 --> 00:18:07,477
We need one fact from linear algebra: the Cholesky decomposition. Any variance matrix V star can be written as L times L transpose. L is lower triangular: all entries above the diagonal are 0. Its diagonal entries are not negative. The left matrix is sigma hat squared V star for the mercury data.

71
00:18:07,927 --> 00:18:15,500
Here is its L, computed from the data. Multiply L by L transpose, and you get the left matrix back.

72
00:18:15,950 --> 00:18:33,934
When the matrix is positive-definite, all diagonal entries of L are positive. Here they are 0.953, 0.498, and 0.872. Then L has an inverse. We use L inverse on the next slide.

73
00:18:34,384 --> 00:18:46,523
The blue cloud shows many possible values of beta star hat minus beta star, drawn for q equal to 2. The entries are correlated, and their spreads are not equal.

74
00:18:46,973 --> 00:18:58,066
Let L be the Cholesky factor of sigma squared V star. It is q by q. Define z as L inverse, times beta star hat minus beta star.

75
00:18:58,516 --> 00:19:08,393
Multiplying by L inverse turns the cloud into a round cloud. The entries of z are not correlated, and each one has variance 1.

76
00:19:08,843 --> 00:19:35,787
Now compute z transpose z. The transpose of L inverse is the inverse of L transpose. L transpose inverse, times L inverse, is the inverse of L L transpose. And L L transpose is sigma squared V star. So z transpose z is 1 over sigma squared, times the quadratic form with V star inverse. This is the numerator of F, without the q.

77
00:19:36,237 --> 00:20:07,235
The slides ask two questions. Why is z N 0 I, and why is z transpose z chi squared? Pause the video and try it. First, z is a linear transformation of a normal vector, so z is normal. Its mean is L inverse times 0, which is 0. Its variance is L inverse, times sigma squared V star, times L inverse transpose. Replace sigma squared V star by L L transpose. Everything cancels to the identity matrix I.

78
00:20:07,685 --> 00:20:21,701
A multivariate normal vector with variance I has independent entries. So z 1 to z q are independent standard normals. The square of each one is chi squared with 1 degree of freedom.

79
00:20:22,151 --> 00:20:41,607
The sum of q independent chi squared 1 variables is chi squared with q degrees of freedom. So under the null hypothesis, the quadratic form over sigma squared has a chi squared distribution with q degrees of freedom. The purple curve shows it for q equal to 3.

80
00:20:42,057 --> 00:20:55,646
From Lecture 7, n minus p plus 1, times sigma hat squared over sigma squared, is chi squared with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. It is independent of beta hat.

81
00:20:56,096 --> 00:21:09,749
Now build the F statistic from two parts. The first part, X 1, is the quadratic form over sigma squared. Under the null hypothesis, it is chi squared with q degrees of freedom.

82
00:21:10,199 --> 00:21:28,738
The second part, X 2, is n minus p plus 1, times sigma hat squared over sigma squared. It is chi squared with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. It is independent of X 1, because sigma hat squared is independent of beta hat.

83
00:21:29,188 --> 00:21:40,537
Recall from Lecture 1: divide each independent chi squared variable by its degrees of freedom, and take the ratio. The ratio has an F distribution.

84
00:21:40,987 --> 00:22:05,350
In the ratio, sigma squared cancels, and the degrees of freedom n minus p plus 1 cancel. What is left is the F statistic: the quadratic form over q times sigma hat squared. Under the null hypothesis, it has an F distribution with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. We can calculate it, because sigma is gone.

85
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:24,445
The slide computes the p-value as an upper tail probability, and asks why. Pause the video and try it. F is never negative. It is a quadratic form with a positive definite matrix, over a positive number. If beta star hat is close to 0, F is small.

86
00:22:24,895 --> 00:22:53,354
A large F means that beta star hat is far from 0, relative to its uncertainty. This is evidence against the null hypothesis. So the p-value is the probability of an F at least as large as the observed one: the upper tail. A small F is not evidence against the null hypothesis. At the 5 percent level with 3 and 130 degrees of freedom, the critical value is 2.674.

87
00:22:53,804 --> 00:23:13,303
These tests apply to any linear regression, not only to categorical covariates. Use a t test for one number: one coefficient, like beta j equal to theta 0. Or one linear combination of coefficients, like beta M minus beta M W equal to 0.

88
00:23:13,753 --> 00:23:24,163
Use an F test for a joint null hypothesis: several coefficients equal to 0 together, or a sub-vector beta star equal to 0.

89
00:23:24,613 --> 00:23:35,728
Both tests work for any covariates. When q is 1, the F test and the two-sided t test give the same answer. Practice question 1 asks you to show this.

90
00:23:36,178 --> 00:23:44,861
Now the R example of Lecture 9. First, the lecture fits the model with fishpart as a number, 0 to 3, and weight.

91
00:23:45,311 --> 00:24:09,396
The slide gives this coefficient table. Each row has the estimate, its standard error, the t value, and the two-sided p-value. The t value is the estimate over the standard error. The degrees of freedom are 135 minus 3, which is 132. The line under the table gives the range and the quartiles of the residuals.

92
00:24:09,846 --> 00:24:29,601
The intercept is minus 10.43. It is the mean MeHg at weight 0 and fishpart 0, far outside the data. The weight slope is 0.1797 per kilogram, with t equal to 5.355 and a very small p-value.

93
00:24:30,051 --> 00:24:49,443
The fishpart estimate is 0.5406, with t equal to 1.690 and p equal to 0.0933. In the picture, this model gives four parallel lines with equal gaps of 0.5406.

94
00:24:49,893 --> 00:25:11,653
So this fit assumes that each step, N to M, M to MW, and MW to W, changes the mean by the same amount. The group means we saw earlier do not follow equal steps. The lecture code says: imagine the code N 0, M 10, MW 11, W 20. The fit would change.

95
00:25:12,103 --> 00:25:21,660
The slide also shows the first rows of the design matrix X, from R. The first column is all 1s. It goes with the intercept.

96
00:25:22,110 --> 00:25:25,417
The second column holds the weight of each subject.

97
00:25:25,867 --> 00:25:36,619
The third column holds the fishpart code as a number. So one coefficient multiplies the code, and each step of one unit adds the same amount to the mean.

98
00:25:37,069 --> 00:25:49,186
Now the lecture recodes fishpart as a categorical variable. The table gives the count of each code: 10 none, 28 muscle, 88 muscle and whole, and 9 whole.

99
00:25:49,636 --> 00:25:56,015
R stores a categorical variable as a factor. A factor has a list of levels, in an order.

100
00:25:56,465 --> 00:26:16,731
By default, R orders the levels alphabetically: muscle, muscle whole, none, whole. R uses the first level as the referent. So with the default order, the referent would be muscle. The lecture code sets the order none, muscle, muscle whole, whole. Then the referent is none, N, as on the slides.

101
00:26:17,181 --> 00:26:40,669
The slide fits both parameterizations in R. Mb is the model with an intercept. Its referent is N. The intercept is beta 0 hat, minus 9.730. The weight slope is 0.1511. The three fishpart rows are beta M hat, beta M W hat, and beta W hat.

102
00:26:41,119 --> 00:26:59,082
Mc is the model without an intercept. The minus 1 in the R formula removes it. Then R uses one indicator for each of the four levels. Its rows are the gamma hats. The weight slope is the same, 0.1511.

103
00:26:59,532 --> 00:27:24,172
Check the relation gamma equals beta 0 plus beta. For M, minus 9.7302 plus 3.0815 is minus 6.6487. This is the fishpartmuscle row of Mc. MW and W agree too. And gamma N hat equals beta 0 hat. The two fits are the same lines.

104
00:27:24,622 --> 00:27:43,161
The slide shows the design matrices of both models, for six subjects. In Mb, the columns are the intercept, weight, and the indicators of M, MW, and W. Subject 102 is in group N, so all three indicators are 0.

105
00:27:43,611 --> 00:27:52,101
In Mc, the columns are weight and the four indicators. Now subject 102 has a 1 in the N column.

106
00:27:52,551 --> 00:28:06,034
So in Mc, each row has exactly one 1 among the indicators. In Mb, a row of the referent group N has no 1 among the indicators. Its mean comes from the intercept only.

107
00:28:06,484 --> 00:28:24,639
The slide compares the fitted values of the two models. The R output gives the range of their differences: from about minus 2 times 10 to the minus 15, to about 1 times 10 to the minus 14. The plot shows each subject's two fitted values.

108
00:28:25,089 --> 00:28:45,953
These differences are only computer rounding error. All points lie on the line Mc equals Mb. So the two parameterizations give the same fitted values, the same residuals, and the same sigma hat. Intervals for a mean response, or for a new observation, are also the same.

109
00:28:46,403 --> 00:29:21,853
The slide uses Mb for intervals. First, the mean MeHg of subjects in group W with weight 70. The row vector x 0 is 1, 70, 0, 0, 1: the intercept, the weight, and the indicators of M, MW, and W. So mu 0 hat is minus 9.7302, plus 0.15105 times 70, plus 3.3736. This is 4.2172, the fit in the R output.

110
00:29:22,303 --> 00:29:50,057
The 95 percent confidence interval is mu 0 hat, plus or minus a margin. The margin is t with 130 degrees of freedom, times sigma hat, times the square root of x 0, X transpose X inverse, x 0 transpose. The standard error is 0.8548, computed from the data. So the interval is 2.526 to 5.908.

111
00:29:50,507 --> 00:30:10,326
Second, group N with weight 60. The fit is minus 0.667, with interval minus 2.395 to 1.061. MeHg cannot be negative. The negative values come from a straight line, outside most of the data of group N.

112
00:30:10,776 --> 00:30:32,557
Third, a prediction interval for one new subject in W with weight 70. Add 1 under the square root, for the new error. The standard error becomes 2.6646. So the prediction interval is minus 1.054 to 9.489. It is much wider than the confidence interval.

113
00:30:33,007 --> 00:30:49,690
The last part of the R example tests whether fishpart is associated with MeHg, in the presence of weight. The null hypothesis is beta star equals 0, with beta star the three fishpart parameters. So q is 3.

114
00:30:50,140 --> 00:31:17,468
The estimate beta star hat is 3.0815, 2.2393, and 3.3736. The code takes the fishpart block of the estimated variance matrix. This block is sigma hat squared times V star. So it already contains the sigma hat squared of the denominator. The red tail shows the 5 percent rejection region, beyond 2.674.

115
00:31:17,918 --> 00:31:39,315
The observed F is 4.007, computed from the data. The slide gives the p-value: 0.009127653. It is the upper tail area of the F distribution with 3 and 130 degrees of freedom, beyond 4.007.

116
00:31:39,765 --> 00:31:52,843
The p-value is less than 0.05. So we reject the null hypothesis. The data give evidence that mean MeHg varies by fishpart, adjusted for weight.

117
00:31:53,293 --> 00:32:10,999
Practice question 1. It is also the exercise on page 41. Test beta j equal to 0 with the t statistic, and with the F statistic. Show that F equals t squared, and that the p-values are the same. Pause the video and try it.

118
00:32:11,449 --> 00:32:26,596
Method 1. The t statistic is beta j hat, over sigma hat times the square root of V j j. Under the null hypothesis, it has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

119
00:32:27,046 --> 00:32:36,305
Method 2. Let beta star be beta j alone. Then q is 1, and V star is the 1 by 1 matrix V j j.

120
00:32:36,755 --> 00:32:57,149
Put these into the F statistic. The numerator is beta j hat, times 1 over V j j, times beta j hat. The denominator is 1 times sigma hat squared. So F is beta j hat squared, over sigma hat squared V j j. This is the square of the t statistic.

121
00:32:57,599 --> 00:33:11,466
Check with the data. For weight in Mb, t is 4.430, and t squared is 19.62. The F statistic for beta 1 alone is also 19.62.

122
00:33:11,916 --> 00:33:21,068
Now the p-values. The t test uses the t distribution with nu degrees of freedom. Here nu is n minus p plus 1.

123
00:33:21,518 --> 00:33:32,633
The two-sided p-value is the probability that the absolute value of T is at least the absolute observed t. This is the area of the two blue tails.

124
00:33:33,083 --> 00:33:55,120
Recall from Lecture 1: T is Z over the square root of X over nu. Square it. T squared is Z squared over 1, divided by X over nu. Z squared is chi squared with 1 degree of freedom, and it is independent of X. So T squared has an F distribution with 1 and nu degrees of freedom.

125
00:33:55,570 --> 00:34:20,317
The F test p-value is the probability that F is at least t observed squared. This is the probability that T squared is at least t observed squared. That is the same as the absolute value of T at least the absolute observed t. So the two p-values are equal. The critical values agree too: 3.914 is 1.978 squared.

126
00:34:20,767 --> 00:34:37,535
Practice question 2. The model has four coefficients, beta 1 to beta 4, and sigma is 10. The question gives X transpose X, and asks for the variance of beta 3 hat minus beta 4 hat. Pause the video and try it.

127
00:34:37,985 --> 00:34:56,097
Look at the zeros. X transpose X has two blocks on the diagonal: A for beta 1 and beta 2, and B for beta 3 and beta 4. The other entries are 0. By the hint, the inverse is block diagonal too, with A inverse and B inverse.

128
00:34:56,547 --> 00:35:10,755
The variance of the difference is sigma squared, times V 3 3 plus V 4 4 minus 2 V 3 4. These three entries are all in the B block of the inverse. So we only need B inverse.

129
00:35:11,205 --> 00:35:46,996
Invert the 2 by 2 block B. Swap the diagonal entries, change the sign of the off-diagonal entries, and divide by the determinant. The determinant is 384 times 543, minus 355 squared. This is 82487. The off-diagonal entry of B is minus 355, so the off-diagonal entry of B inverse is plus 355 over 82487.

130
00:35:47,446 --> 00:36:07,073
So V 3 3 is 543 over 82487. V 4 4 is 384 over 82487. V 3 4 is 355 over 82487.

131
00:36:07,523 --> 00:36:33,955
Now put them together. 543 plus 384 minus 710 is 217. Multiply by sigma squared, which is 100, and divide by 82487. The variance of beta 3 hat minus beta 4 hat is 0.2631. The positive covariance makes the variance of the difference smaller.

132
00:36:34,405 --> 00:36:52,111
To recap. A numeric code for the categories forces fixed mean differences. Use indicator variables instead. The gamma form has one mean for each level. The beta form has the referent mean beta 0, and differences from it. They are the same model.

133
00:36:52,561 --> 00:37:02,631
With weight in the model, each level has its own line, and the lines are parallel. beta M is the difference between M and N at the same weight.

134
00:37:03,081 --> 00:37:11,913
To test one parameter or one linear combination, use a t test. For a difference, the variance includes the covariance term.

135
00:37:12,363 --> 00:37:33,760
To test several parameters together, use the F test. Under the null hypothesis, F has an F distribution with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. The p-value is the upper tail. With q equal to 1, F is t squared. The next video adds interactions: lines with different slopes.
